# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A contact-edge common lattice neighbor

**Date:** 2026-09-04  
**Branch:** `research/target-c-c97-caterpillar-fan-capacity-2026-09-04`  
**Run type:** exact sharpening of the Section 173 right-shore shield  
**Target:** locked three-channel Schur sector branch  
**Status:** LEVEL I STILL OPEN

## 1. Equality extension of the Section 164 common-neighbor theorem

Let `Lambda` be the actual response triangular lattice. The common placement chooses its phase generic for the finite response set `X^*`: no response point lies on the lattice and no response-point-to-lattice distance is exactly one.

For a response point `x`, put

`N_Lambda(x)={y in Lambda: |x-y|<1}`.

Section 164.1 proves more geometrically than its strict-distance statement records. If `x` is generic and

`U_x = union_{y in N_Lambda(x)} B(y,1)`,

then its triangle/lozenge cell proof gives

`dist(x, R^2\U_x) >= 1`.

This extends to a unit-distance partner provided that partner is also lattice-generic.

### Theorem CN1 — common strict lattice neighbor on a unit contact

Let `x,a` both be generic for the same unit triangular lattice and suppose

`|x-a|=1`.

Then

`N_Lambda(x) cap N_Lambda(a) != empty`.

### Proof

Assume `a notin U_x`. Since `a` lies in the complement and `|x-a|=1`, the Section-164 distance estimate is sharp at `a`.

If `a` were an interior point of the complement, a sufficiently small displacement from `a` toward `x` would remain in the complement while having distance strictly below one from `x`, contradicting

`dist(x,R^2\U_x)>=1`.

Hence `a` lies on `partial U_x`.

But the boundary of `U_x` is contained in the union of the unit circles centered at the finitely many sites in `N_Lambda(x)`. Thus some `y in N_Lambda(x)` satisfies

`|a-y|=1`,

contradicting genericity of `a`.

Therefore `a in U_x`, so for some `y in N_Lambda(x)` one has `|a-y|<1`. This `y` lies in both strict neighborhoods. QED.

The strict-generic response phase needed here is already available in the Target A common placement.

## 2. Connected contact shields cost at most three new lattice sites per vertex

Let `Sigma` be a connected unit-contact graph in `X^*`, and let the Section 173 right-shore shield be

`R_Sigma = (union_{q in V(Sigma)} N_Lambda(q)) cap Y_in`.

Every generic response vertex has three or four strict lattice neighbors, so

`|N_Lambda(q)|<=4`.

Choose a spanning-tree order `q_1,...,q_v` in which every `q_j`, `j>1`, has an earlier parent joined to it by a unit contact edge. Theorem CN1 gives a common strict lattice neighbor of `q_j` and its parent. Therefore

`|N_Lambda(q_j) \ union_{i<j}N_Lambda(q_i)| <=3`.

Starting with at most four sites gives:

### Corollary CN2 — connected contact-shield bound

`|R_Sigma| <= 3|V(Sigma)|+1`.

This improves the generic Section 173 union bound `4|V(Sigma)|` without any assumption on contact-edge direction.

## 3. The locked fan improves to exactly three sites per fan vertex

For the Section 172 locked fan, the unique cycle is the locked unit equilateral triangle `T` and all remaining contact edges form three vertex-disjoint channel trees attached to its three vertices.

First bound the lattice neighborhood of the three triangle vertices. If `y,y'` are any two lattice sites lying in

`R_T = union_{q in V(T)} N_Lambda(q)`,

choose triangle vertices `q,q'` with

`|y-q|<1`, `|y'-q'|<1`.

The diameter of a unit equilateral triangle is one, so

`|y-y'| < 1+|q-q'|+1 <=3`.

Partition the triangular lattice into the nine cosets of the index-nine sublattice `3Lambda`. Distinct points in one coset are at distance at least three. Hence the strict-diameter estimate above allows at most one point from each coset:

`|R_T|<=9`.

Now grow the three channel trees away from the triangle. Every added response vertex is joined by a unit contact edge to an already present vertex, so Theorem CN1 says its strict lattice neighborhood contributes at most three new sites. If the full fan has `v` vertices, there are `v-3` added vertices. Therefore

### Theorem CN3 — locked-fan right-shore shield

`|R_Sigma| <= 9+3(v-3)=3v`.

## 4. Improved locked Schur endpoint

Combine CN3 with the contracted contact-shield Hall theorem

`zeta >= delta_Sigma-|R_Sigma|`.

For the three-channel locked fan,

`zeta >= delta_fan-3|V(Sigma)|`.

Thus the locked Target A branch closes whenever

`delta_fan >= s+|R_Sigma|`,

and in particular it is enough to prove the explicit bound

`delta_fan >= s+3|V(Sigma)|`.

The promoted Section 173 route originally paid up to `5|V(Sigma)|` after including contact vertices; matching contraction reduced this to `4|V(Sigma)|`; the common-neighbor theorem reduces the locked-fan right-shore cost further to `3|V(Sigma)|`.

This still does not prove the required sector Hall deficiency.
